      "report_date": "2026-11-24",
      "title": "Exposing the Gmail “Zero-Day” Behind Remus Stealer’s Email Interception Feature",
      "summary": "Remus Stealer's developers advertised a Gmail email interception feature in November 2026, claiming it was a Gmail zero-day that persists even after password changes and session revocation. Gen Threat Labs analyzed the feature and found it is a native DLL module that silently installs a malicious Chrome extension, disguised as Google Sheets, by tampering with Chrome's preferences files. The extension polls Gmail's Atom feed for new unread messages across up to ten signed-in accounts, downloads each full message via the attachment view endpoint, and sends the content to the C2 server. The researchers concluded there is no Gmail vulnerability, since the extension relies entirely on the victim's valid session and stops working once sessions are revoked. Defenders should watch for unexpected extensions and Developer Mode enabled in Chrome, because the real risk is that victims often overlook the malicious extension after changing passwords.",
