      "title": "How Hackers Use WhatsApp and Telegram to Hijack Windows PCs",
      "summary": "WatchGuard Red Cell researcher Euler Neto analyzed four malware campaigns targeting Windows users in Southeast Asia, particularly Cambodia and Malaysia, distributed through Telegram and WhatsApp. The Telegram-based campaigns deliver remote access trojans, including a suspected Gh0stRAT variant and a Go-based RAT capable of surveillance, data theft, and cryptocurrency hijacking, using techniques such as DLL sideloading, PNG-concealed payloads, and process injection. The WhatsApp-based campaigns abuse Zoho's ManageEngine Endpoint Central to gain unauthorized remote control of infected devices. The report notes that Chinese-language references do not by themselves establish attribution to a specific threat actor. Its key takeaway for defenders is that trusted applications do not guarantee trusted behavior, so monitoring application behavior and remote access activity is essential.",
